From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Er Gibbio <gibbio@pm.me>
Date: Tue, 20 Oct 2026 20:36:20 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 11/11] display: report the display delay to servers that send
 their real time

spice-server 0.16 ("Send real time to client") no longer runs the client's
mm-time MM_TIME_DELTA (400 ms) behind its own clock and leaves the playout
delay to the client. Against the session clock every frame then arrives late
by its encoding and network time (~30 ms on our LAN), even though the playout
clock shows it ~50 ms later. We reported that negative delay in the stream
reports, and the server's MJPEG rate control treats anything below -15 ms as
a client that cannot keep up: in our test it cut the stream to ~1 Mbit/s with
397 "stream data too late" frames in 40 s, visibly choppy and blurry.

Report, and count in the stream stats, the later of the session-clock delay
and the delay the frame really gets before it is shown. With older servers
the session-clock delay (~350 ms) is the later one, so what they see does not
change.
---
 src/channel-display.c | 11 ++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 10 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/src/channel-display.c b/src/channel-display.c
index 13deae1..7169484 100644
--- a/src/channel-display.c
+++ b/src/channel-display.c
@@ -2088,6 +2088,15 @@ static void display_handle_stream_data(SpiceChannel *channel, SpiceMsgIn *in)
     shift = display_playout_shift(channel, margin);
     margin -= shift;
     st->shown_margin = margin;
+    /* spice-server 0.16 sends its real time instead of running our clock
+     * MM_TIME_DELTA (400 ms) behind it, and leaves the delay to the client: a
+     * frame then arrives already late on the session clock, by the encoding and
+     * network time, although it is shown `margin` ms from now. Report (and count
+     * in the stats) the later of the two. Older servers keep seeing the session
+     * clock they set up, newer ones the delay the frame really gets, rather than a
+     * client that seems unable to keep up: their rate control cuts the stream
+     * whenever the reported delay is below -15 ms (to ~1 Mbit/s in our test). */
+    margin_report = MAX(margin_report, margin);
     if (margin > 0) {
         SpiceSession *s = spice_channel_get_session(channel);
 
@@ -2096,7 +2105,7 @@ static void display_handle_stream_data(SpiceChannel *channel, SpiceMsgIn *in)
             margin = 0;
         }
     }
-    display_stream_stats_save(st, op->multi_media_time, mmtime);
+    display_stream_stats_save(st, op->multi_media_time, op->multi_media_time - margin_report);
 
     /* Let the video decoder queue the frames so it can optimize their
      * decoding and best decide if/when to drop them when they are late,
